      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Budgeting: Separating Model Weights, KV Cache, and Runtime Needs",
      "body": "When planning to run LLMs locally, especially on hardware with limited VRAM, a systematic approach to VRAM allocation is key. This guide outlines a hypothetical checklist, explicitly noting that no specific models or hardware were tested; this is a conceptual framework for planning.\n\nWe can divide VRAM usage into three primary categories: \n1.  **Model Weights:** The fundamental parameters of the LLM. Larger models require more VRAM.\n2.  **KV Cache:** Essential for inference, its size scales with context length and batch size. Longer contexts mean a larger KV cache.\n3.  **Other Runtime Allocations:** This includes intermediate activations, framework overhead (like PyTorch or TensorFlow), and any other dynamic memory needs during operation.\n\nFor a hypothetical 12 GB GPU, understanding these distinct allocations can help prevent Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors. For example, a model with fewer parameters but a very long context might still exceed VRAM due to a large KV cache. This hypothetical breakdown aims to facilitate more informed VRAM budgeting before attempting to load a model.",
